     9	--- Page 2 ---
    10	Systematic Reviews in Requirements Engineering:
    11	A Tertiary Study
    12	Muneera Bano, Didar Zowghi
    13	Faculty of Engineering and IT
    14	 University of Technology, Sydney
    15	Sydney, Australia
    16	[Muneera.Bano, Didar.Zowghi]@uts.edu.au  Naveed Ikram
    17	Ibn-e-Sina Empirical Software Engineering Lab
    18	Riphah International University
    19	Islamabad, Pakistan
    20	naveed.ikram@riphah.edu.pk
    21
    22
    23	Abstract —There has been an increasing interest in
    24	conducting Systematic Literature Reviews (SLR) amon g
    25	Requirements Engineering (RE) researchers in recent  years.
    26	However, so far there have been no tertiary studies  conducted
    27	to provide a comprehensive overview of these publis hed SLR
    28	in RE. In this paper we present a tertiary study of  SLR that
    29	focus solely on RE related topics by following the guidelines of
    30	Evidence Based Software Engineering. We have conduc ted
    31	both automated search of major online sources and m anual
    32	search of the RE and SLR related conferences and jo urnals.
    33	Our tertiary study has identified 53 distinct syste matic
    34	reviews published from 2006 to 2014 and reported in  64
    35	publications.  We have assessed the resulting SLR f or their
    36	quality, and coverage of specific RE related topics  thus
    37	identifying some gaps. We have observed that the qu ality of
    38	SLR in RE has been decreasing over the recent years . There is
    39	a strong need to replicate some of these SLR to inc rease the
    40	reliability of their results for future RE research .
    41
    42	Index Terms — Requirements Engineering, Mapping Study,
    43	Systematic Literature Review, Tertiary Study
    44	I. INTRODUCTION
    45	In early 1990’s, RE emerged as a discipline of its own
    46	and dedicated conferences (e.g. ICRE, ISRE, REFSQ) and a
    47	journal (REJ) were established to promote the resea rch in
    48	RE [1]. RE research community has made significant
    49	progress on various fronts and has accumulated a la rge body
    50	of knowledge to deliver new processes, tools, techn iques,
    51	methods and frameworks [2]. Attempts have been made
    52	with traditional reviews (e.g. [1, 2]) as well as s ystematic
    53	reviews to aggregate and present the state of the a rt on
    54	various RE topics, e.g. Elicitation Techniques [3],
    55	Requirements change and Evolution [4, 5], and user related
    56	issues [6].
    57	Systematic Literature Reviews are secondary studies  that
    58	provide a way of synthesizing the available primary  studies
    59	in empirical literature on a particular topic [7]. SLR is
    60	distinguished from a traditional review as it follo ws a
    61	rigorous and scrupulous procedure for search and se lection
    62	of the sample studies in review. It is methodical a nd
    63	meticulous process of collecting and collating the acceptable
    64	quality published empirical studies based on a syst emic
    65	protocol to reduce bias and provide transparency to  the process. The process is formally documented and hen ce
    66	repeatable. There are three types of SLR [7, 8]:
    67	Conventional SLR:  they provide review of a focused and
    68	fine-grained research question(s) and would employ
    69	quantitative assessment on the results form primary  studies.
    70	Systematic Mapping Studies:  that aim to find and classify
    71	the studies in a specific area and have a broader r esearch
    72	question. They are helpful in identifying available  primary
    73	studies before conducting SLR. They use the same se arch
    74	and selection procedure as conventional SLR but the ir focus
    75	is on the classification of the resulting studies t o provide a
    76	map of the research in that area. Tertiary study: An SLR
    77	that aggregates the data and information from a num ber of
    78	existing SLR on a specific topic or area of researc h is called
    79	as Tertiary study [8].
    80	A tertiary study aims to provide information about the
    81	available published SLR on a specific topic and tab ulate the
    82	information regarding these SLR, e.g. the number of  SLR
    83	published, their quality and the focus of those SLR  within
    84	the topic of interest. Various tertiary studies hav e been
    85	published in software engineering recently e.g. [8- 11], but to
    86	the best of our knowledge none of them focus on
    87	aggregating the SLR for RE.
    88	In this paper we present for the first time a terti ary study
    89	that aims to identify all the SLR published about R E related
    90	topics by following EBSE guidelines [7]. Our object ive is to
    91	present the topics covered by these SLR and the num ber of
    92	primary studies found in those SLR to create a rich er picture
    93	of the empirical research in RE thus far. Our study  has
    94	identified 53 distinct Systematic Reviews in RE rel ated
    95	areas published from 2006 to 2014 and reported in 6 4
    96	publications. Our tertiary study reported in this p aper is also
    97	the first step in our larger research plan for cont ributing to
    98	an updated roadmap of RE following the roadmaps
    99	published in 2000 [1] and 2007 [2].
   100	Section II gives details of the Systematic Review
   101	Planning and Execution. Section III presents the re sults of
   102	review process and discusses our answers to the res earch
   103	questions. Section IV describes limitations of the results,
   104	and section VI states the conclusion and suggests f uture
   105	works.
   106
   107	--- Page 3 ---
   108	II. SYSTEMATIC MAPPING TERTIARY STUDY
   109	According to the EBSE guidelines there are three ma in
   110	phases of a Systematic Review [7], planning, execut ion and
   111	reporting results.
   112	A. Planning
   113	During the planning phase we developed a formal
   114	protocol containing the details of the strategies f or search
   115	and selection process, quality assessment, data ext raction,
   116	data synthesis and data analysis. Our tertiary stud y was
   117	guided by three research questions:
   118
   119	RQ1: What are the main areas of research in RE that  have
   120	been covered by published SLR?
   121	RQ2: What is the quality of the published SLR in RE  like?
   122	RQ3: What are the gaps in the coverage of RE resear ch
   123	topics in the published SLR?
   124
   125	To answer RQ1, based on the publication titles and
   126	abstracts, we identified topics of SLR and grouped similar
   127	studies together under the same category. The reaso n for
   128	this exercise was not only to identify the coverage  but also
   129	to identify gaps in the RE research (RQ3). The numb er of
   130	primary studies identified by these SLR shows the c overage
   131	of empirical work within their area of focus in RE.
   132	To answer RQ2, we assessed both the quality of the SLR
   133	and the quality of the publications. We used the qu ality
   134	assessment checklist from previously published tert iary
   135	studies (e.g. [8, 9, 11]) for evaluating the SLR as  a whole
   136	(grouping multiple publications together where appl icable).
   137	These tertiary studies have utilized the criteria d efined by
   138	the Centre for Reviews and Dissemination (CDR) Data base
   139	of Abstracts of Reviews of Effects (DARE), of the Y ork
   140	University. It includes four questions for assessin g quality
   141	of an SLR as shown in Table I. The quality assessme nt
   142	criteria were applied on the whole study rather tha n on
   143	every single publication from that study due to the  fact that
   144	all required information may not be found in a sing le
   145	publication especially if it is a conference or a w orkshop
   146	paper due to space limitation.
