   433	RQ2: What is the quality of the published SLR in RE  like?
   434	We applied the Quality Assessment criteria describe d in
   435	Table I on all the studies. Figure 2 shows the dist ribution of the
   436	number of studies in different quality scores. Out of 53 we
   437	were able to retrieve 51 studies on which we could apply
   438	quality criteria (as we couldn’t find full sources of S3 and S8).
   439	42 studies out of 51 have scored 2 or above out of 4. Figure 3
   440	represents the number of studies classified accordi ng to the
   441	scores for each quality assessment question (see Ta ble I).
   442
   443	Fig. 2.  Quality score distribution of the resulting SLR
   444	0510 15 20 25 30 35 40 45
   445	QA1 QA2 QA3 QA4 0
   446	0.5
   447	1
   448
   449	Fig. 3.  Quality score distribution for each QA check from T able I
   450	Table VI shows the most highly cited publications. For
   451	citations of all the studies see Appendix A. Regard less of the
   452	quality score, the highly cited papers are the ones  published in
   453	prestigious conferences and journals related to RE and SLR
   454	community (i.e. RE, REJ, IST). S2[A] from Alan Davi s et. al.
   455	published in 2006 has been cited 154 times showing a
   456	significant interest in the results of their SLR fr om RE research
   457	community. Figure 4 shows the average of the qualit y scores of
   458	the publication over the span of the years of their  publication.
   459	Although in all years the quality of the SLR has av erage score
   460	of above 2, there is a decrease in quality score af ter 2009 with
   461	the increase in the number of SLR studies being pub lished
   462	(Figure 2 and 4).
   463	In previously published tertiary studies [8, 9], it  was
   464	observed that the average quality scores of all the  SLR in
   465	Software Engineering has increased over the years. According
   466	to Figure 1, for SLR in RE there is a sudden increa se in the
   467	publications in 2009, but if we look at Figure 4, t he average
   468	quality of the published SLR has decreased starting  from the
   469	same year. One of the reasons in the decrease of th e quality can
   470	be inferred from Figure 3. It shows that over half of the SLR have ignored to assess the quality of the included primary
   471	studies (QA3) and also neglected to provide an over view or
   472	summary of the included primary studies (QA4). The
   473	distinguishing factor of an SLR against a tradition al literature
   474	review is the rigor of process but if the researche rs would
   475	overlook this aspect then the results would not be as reliable
   476	and useful to the RE community. This is a serious i ssue worthy
   477	of further consideration because if the quality of the included
   478	primary studies is not assessed then there can be s erious threats
   479	to the validity of the results produced by those SL R.
   480
   481	Fig. 4.  Average of Quality Scores against the year of publi cation of SLR
   482	TABLE VI.  TOP 10  HIGHLY CITED SLR
