   490	S50 43 ITSE 3
   491	S15 42 CSI 2.5
   492	S9 41 IST 4
   493	S46 41 ESEM 1.5
   494	RQ3: What are the gaps in the coverage of RE resear ch
   495	topics in the published SLR?
   496	In answering this questions we were interested in t hree
   497	types of gaps in the SLR on RE. First, we considere d the
   498	anomalies and potential conflicts in the reported n umber of
   499	primary studies in the SLR on the same topic by dif ferent
   500	authors in different years. Second, we were concern ed about
   501	the small number of reported primary studies in som e of the
   502	SLR. Finally, we observed some of the well known ar eas of RE
   503	research that seem to have been neglected by resear chers who
   504	have conducted SLR.
   505	1) Anomalies: In some cases, there are doubts about the
   506	number of primary studies included in the SLR as th ey are not
   507	providing a consistent view. For example, in Table V, for
   508	Requirements Prioritization, S1 has reported 8 prim ary studies
   509	from 1996 to 2005, whereas S4 has reported 240 prim ary
   510	studies from 1996 to 2007. It is indeed very surpri sing to have
   511	found additional 232 published empirical studies on
   512	requirements prioritization in a span of just two y ears. When
   513	we look at the procedure followed by these two SLR (S1 and
   514	S4), although both of them were looking for empiric al studies
   515	on requirements prioritization but they have search ed with
   516	different search terms in their search strings, cov ered different
   517
   518	--- Page 7 ---
   519	number of data bases (S1 has searched 8 sources whe reas S4
   520	has searched 14) and devised different inclusion/ex clusion
   521	criteria. This raises concerns on the validity of S LR process
   522	adopted by S1 and also on the usefulness of the res ults for
   523	future RE researchers if they do not provide a comp lete and
   524	correct coverage within their area of focus.
   525	Another example is the SLR on the state of the art in RE
   526	(S24) that has included 242 studies from the entire  body of the
   527	empirical RE research literature. However, in Table  V we see
   528	that S4 has 240, S21 has 364 and S25 has 281 primar y studies
   529	on specific topics within RE. If a researcher is fo cusing on a
   530	specific topic within RE, then the number of includ ed primary
   531	studies reported should have been a subset of the p rimary
   532	studies that were included in the state-of-the-art for the entire
   533	RE research literature (S24). The fact that this is  not the case in
   534	these examples raises serious concerns both about t he strict
   535	compliance to the EBSE guidelines as well as the re liable
   536	coverage of the published literature. There is a st rong need for
   537	replicated studies on the similar topics to verify these results. If
   538	the protocol for the conduct of SLR is readily avai lable, then
   539	replication of studies should be straightforward.
